From: Max More <more@chaph.usc.edu>
Date: Mon, 5 Dec 1994 09:44:11 -0800 (PST)
Subject: [#94-12-163] FEMME: "Sleeping" for success

[This was supposed to go to the List several days ago, but somehow
never appeared.]

Geoff:
> Making few compromises means she does what she wants when she wants.
> I'm sure she slept with many people, for both power and pleasure. I respect
> her for that, too. Believe me, if it meant becoming a rich mega-star I could 
> do the same and enjoy it.
> 
> It's an economic transaction. Can you imagine having something so valuable
> that you could trade a half-hour's use of it for millions of dollars? Would
> you let your dignity get in the way?

Pointing out that it's an economic transaction hardly suffices to make it 
desirable. Personally, the thought of having sex or faking a relationship with
someone just to advance my career repels me. It's no different from bribing to
get what you want. If you then achieve your objective of wealth and success, 
you'll know that some of it was not due to relevant talents and efforts but to
manipulation. If you wanted to win a marathon, or a chess game, would you 
feel so good about winning if you had sex with your revival in exchange for 
their dropping out?

> 
> Ferengi Law of Aquisition:
> Dignity and an empty sack is worth: an empty sack.

I'll keep my dignity thank you, and fill my sack maybe a little more slowly 
but with self-respect and satisfaction.

Simon:
> >Also, you should keep in mind the statistical fact that, as something
> >grows more popular, it gravitates more toward the mean, and further
> >away from extremes like Extropianism.
> 
Geoff:
> Does that include Extropianism? [A very relevant thought to me, as part of
> Team FQA. Since Dv/Dt, Mistress Romana and I are working on a book
> designed to make Extropianism more popular.]

Statistically it must be true that "as something grows more popular, it 
gravitates more toward the mean". That doesn't mean that as an idea becomes 
more popular it must be getting weaker or less "pure". The statistical truth 
can be maintained also if the mean is moving towards the earlier extreme. As 
Extropianism becomes more popular or widespread ("popular" can be 
ambiguous), probably some people will adopt softened versions of it. But it 
may also be that may people become radicalized. Look at the Islamic 
fundamentalist revival in Iran, or the rise of scientific rationalism in the 
west in the 18th & 18th centuries: These ideologies didn't weaken as they 
were more widely accepted; they shifted increasing number of people to what 
used to be an extreme view held by very few.

Onward!

