From: fhapgood@world.std.com (Fred Hapgood)
Date: Mon, 5 Dec 1994 17:28:57 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-169] Machinery in Russia

The closest thing to the anarcho-capitalist utopia -- a society
in which there are no state-enforced rights -- on the horizon is
Russia.  That's a society in which the sphere of permissible
behavior is defined almost entirely by the intersection of the
money spent to promote it with the money spent to inhibit it.

So what do anarcho-capitalists think about contemporary Russia?
Do they think the country has inadvertently stumbled onto the
next path of social progress?  Do they think the press has
overstated the downside of letting that society be run by private
groups?  If the powers of the Russian police were to be
reasserted and the mafia reduced to the pitiable condition it is
in the US, would that be a step backward? In fact, what do
anarcho-capitalists think of the mafia in the US?  Do they feel
it has been unjustly persecuted, and right-thinking people ought
to come to the mafia's defense?  If not, how come?




