From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Mon, 5 Dec 1994 14:56:28 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-170] FEMME: "Sleeping" for success

At 2:13 PM 12/5/94, Max More wrote:
>Personally, the thought of having sex or faking a relationship with
>someone just to advance my career repels me. It's no different from bribing to
>get what you want.

Hey, what's wrong with bribery?  In situations where the market has been
distorted by statist interference, bribery is often the only way to give
market forces an opportunity to reallocate the resource.  Remember, "free"
means "you can't buy it" -- a special case of price-fixing.  For example,
online services that don't charge for connect time create no incentive for
users to limit the length of their online sessions, and so a tragedy of the
commons frequently emerges.  Witness the recent ExI-Bay discussions of
Netcom's inability to keep performance on a par with the expansion of its
subscriber base -- everything on Netcom, from ftp accessibility to the
alacrity of mail delivery, suffers because there's no mechanism for
bringing market forces to bear on the problem.

I wouldn't hesitate to bribe anybody to get anything that I would expect to
be able to buy in a free market (or wouldn't need, like passports, visas,
concealed-carry permits, and the like).  If the bribe was in the form of
sex, I would make sure that what I was getting was worth what I was giving
-- I value my sex appeal highly (see
ftp://ftp.netcom.com/pub/dk/dkrieger/towel.gif) and wouldn't sell myself
cheaply -- but I wouldn't otherwise differentiate between sex and any other
coin at my disposal.

>If you then achieve your objective of wealth and success,
>you'll know that some of it was not due to relevant talents and efforts but to
>manipulation. If you wanted to win a marathon, or a chess game, would you
>feel so good about winning if you had sex with your revival in exchange for
>their dropping out?

That depends on what you wanted to win for.  If you wanted recognition of
your abilities, particularly in your own mind, then winning through bribery
would be self-defeating.  If you wanted the trophy for some other reason --
I'm at a loss to think of any at the moment; maybe there's important
microfilm concealed in the first-place medal or something -- and it was
worth more to than you had to pay for it, I don't see the problem.  (BTW,
sex requires talent and effort, too -- especially if you expect to get
value in exchange for it!)

The converse is true, as well.  The rival who gives up the trophy for a
roll in the hay must have wanted sex more than the title -- else s/he
wouldn't have accepted the bargain.  A judge in an athletic event who
allows hirself to be paid off is selling the same reputation capital as a
criminal judge taking the same graft.  The judge must value the money more
highly than hir repuatation, and will suffer in the market for judgeship
later on because of it if the bribe is found out.  In the long run,
dishonest judges are selected out of the market, and further incentives are
engineered to prevent bribery, but it always comes down to the relative
evaluation by the economic agent with something -- a medal, a reputation,
or sex -- to sell.

[Quoting Geoff Dale:]
>> Ferengi Law of Aquisition:
>> Dignity and an empty sack is worth: an empty sack.
>
>I'll keep my dignity thank you, and fill my sack maybe a little more slowly
>but with self-respect and satisfaction.
>Max More        more@extropy.org        more@usc.edu
>President       Extropy Institute (ExI)

Fine; you place a higher value upon your reputation capital than others
might.  Getting back to the topic at hand, Madonna risked very little
reputation capital in sleeping her way to the top -- Has the "McDonna"
epithet damaged her marketability and earning power one iota?  Probably
not.  Sexual chastity (or even sexual continence) is not relevant to her
professional reputation as someone who can SELL RECORDS.

To my mind, selling sex is no different than selling any other commodity
you may have.

"Faking a relationship" is the one thing Max mentions that I also find
wrong, because it implies failure to fulfill a contract.  If both parties
recognize and agree on the finite and voluntary nature of the exchange --
that the sex-seller can stop paying sex when s/he no longer needs what the
sex-buyer is providing in return -- I don't see a problem with "playing
house" either.
